@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        Christ hath ordained [the sacraments], that by them he might set before our eyes the mysteries of our 
        salvation, and might more strongly confirm the faith which we have in his blood, and might seal his grace 
        in our hearts.
    </span>
    <span>Bishop John Jewel, A Treatise of the Sacraments</span>
</div>

<ol>
    <li>
        Sacraments are the means by which God communicates, upholds, demonstrates, and actually 
        bestows grace upon His people.
    </li>
    <li>
        Just as Christ is one person in two natures, the divine and the human, so too are Christ's sacraments
        one promise in two elements, the earthly sign and that which is signified in heaven.
    </li>
    <li>
        In baptism, the sign is water and the signified is spiritual death and resurrection with Christ. 
        In the Lord's supper, the sign is bread and wine and the signified is the body and blood of Christ.
    </li>
    <li>
        The sign and signified are unfailingly bound together by
        the work of the Holy Spirit. As surely as we see the earthly sign, so too do we see the Spirit
        at work on our souls. In this way heaven
        and earth touch in the sacraments, and the great power of heaven is set before our eyes
        for all to behold.
    </li>
    <li>
        The sign and the signified cannot be mixed or confused, as if the bread transforms into Christ or
        the pouring of water is the Holy Spirit. Rather the two are distinct: that which is earthly is 
        attributed to the sign, and that which is heavenly to the signified.
    </li>
    <li>
        But neither do we separate the sign and the signified, as if there is "Spirit baptism" and
        a "water baptism", or an "eating of bread" and a separate "eating of Christ". The promises of God have
        made the earthly and heavenly elements inseparable, so that when we speak of a sacrament we refer
        to the one promise instead of the individual elements. As Saint Paul instructs,
        there "is one Lord, one faith, one baptism."
    </li>
    <li>
        Because the sacraments are one, it is proper to attribute to them the workings of both the
        sign and the signified, both the earthly and the heavenly elements. In this way we rightly say that 
        baptism saves, and in the eucharist
        we feast on Christ.
    </li>
    <li>
        Like all other evangelical graces, the heavenly reality signified in the sacraments is received and made
        effectual by faith.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />